Opaque means opaque, one dial, and a task list
===============================================

Four files, one story: the transparency setting tells the truth, and first run stops asking
questions it has already been answered.

**The window is not declared transparent any more.** A window declared to the compositor as an
ARGB surface could not be made fully opaque from the stylesheet — measured, 15 of 255 levels of
the backdrop came through regardless of the colour painted, identical over white and over black.
That residue belongs to the surface, not to CSS, so a dial reading 100% over it was lying. It is an
ordinary opaque window now, which is also what stock upstream builds.

**The dial moves the window.** With an opaque window a stylesheet-only dial had nothing to reveal:
the panel colour and the background are within a few levels of each other, so 100% and 40% looked
identical. The slider also writes `windowOpacity` through AppPreferences, and the app applies it
with `gdk_window_set_opacity` — `_NET_WM_WINDOW_OPACITY` on X11, a no-op on Wayland where the
surfaces still move over the app's own backdrop. At 100% the property is removed entirely, so 100
is stock opaque rather than almost.

**Two themes became one, plus a voice.** Matrix and Retro were nearly the same greens; two themes
that look alike are one theme and a second name. That slot now carries `volkdude85`: the retro
palette with the app's own copy instead of the neutral default. A brand-new install reads like the
app unless that theme is chosen, and the "Just give me the table" shortcut is gone from the empty
state — that belongs to setup, not to a new instance.

**Onboarding is gated on having been used.** The flag alone is not enough: a preferences file can
be reset, migrated, or shipped empty into an install that already has weeks of conversation in it,
and re-asking somebody who has been using the app for a month is the fastest way to look broken. If
there is history, there is no wizard.

**Tasks.** A page in the sidebar, numbered from zero, backed by `tasks.json` through
`/api/quixi/tasks` and `/api/quixi/tasks/update`. Status cycles on click. The number is the point:
"task 3" has to keep meaning the same task, so the list does not renumber when a status changes.

**Affordance.** The two card actions in first run ("Use a local model", "Use a cloud provider")
were drawn with the quiet variant — no background, no border — so they only became a box under the
pointer; they carry a real surface now. Selects get a drawn chevron and a hover border, because the
engine's own arrow on a dark field is close to invisible, and the provider list always contains at
least Ollama Cloud and Custom endpoint so the dropdown is never an empty box while the catalog is
in flight.

